    - security  # 72bd
    isbefore: []  # 72bd
    duplicate_of: []  # 72bd
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 72bd
    pr_ref:  # 72bd
    description: "Repro, live on hypergumbo's own tree: adding io_primitives: [io-primitives-overlays/hypergumbo-self.yaml] (language: python) to docs/hypergumbo.claims.yaml makes the census abort with 'declares language python but was loaded for javascript'. hypergumbo's repo contains javascript, so its OWN overlay cannot be wired into its OWN claims file. Blocks the owner's 2026-08-15 ruling that hypergumbo should ship a third-party overlay as a user example. Also means the two overlays already shipped under docs/io-primitives-overlays/ (python-http-clients, go-web-frameworks) can never be declared together, which is the obvious thing a polyglot repo would do. THE FIX MUST DISCRIMINATE, not just soften: an overlay declaring a language that names a SHIPPED catalogue but not this one is simply not applicable and should be skipped; an overlay declaring a language no catalogue knows (language: pyton) is a typo and must still raise, because silently ignoring it leaves the author believing their rows applied - the fail-quiet direction."  # 72bd
    fields:  # 72bd
      statement: "Declaring a project-local io_primitives overlay must not fail a run because the repo contains a language the overlay does not describe. VIOLATED: extra_catalogs.io_primitives paths are passed to load_catalog for EVERY language present, and load_catalog raises IoPrimitiveOverlayError on any language mismatch, so declaring one python overlay aborts verify-claims on any repo that also contains javascript."  # 72bd
      root_cause: "The language-mismatch check in load_catalog's overlay loop is unconditional. It was written for the CLI --io-primitives flag, where a mismatch really is a user error worth refusing, and never distinguished that case from the claims-file case where the same path list is fanned out across every language in the tree. One overlay list, two call shapes, one answer."  # 72bd
